.TH LSDEV 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
lsdev \- list the devices on the bus
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsdev
.RB [ \-i ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsdev
lists the devices on the computer's bus, as a table with a header row:
.TP
.B NAME
The kernel's name for it: its type and its place among devices of that
type in bus order
.RB ( terminal0 ,
.BR storage1 ).
A device with data to read or write (the EEPROM) has a file by that name
in
.IR /dev .
.TP
.B TYPE
What kind of device it is.
.TP
.B PATH
Its slot address, as
.B sata:0
or
.BR exp:2 ;
.B \-
for a device that is not in a slot (a cabled screen, a card in a hub).
.TP
.B ID
Its id address,
.BI id: uuid ,
which names it however it is connected. Shown only with
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B LABEL
The name the player gave its item, if any.
.PP
Rows are in bus order. The list is the kernel's: it reads the bus when
the bus says it changed (or a device it called is gone), so just after a
change it may be a moment behind.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i
Add the
.B ID
column.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on any other argument.
.SH NOTES
Bus positions (the numbers in the case view) are not addresses; programs
name devices by their path or id.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR df (1)
